<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kongo Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1665">1665</a>–<a href="/years/1709">1709</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> Civil War was a war of succession in the Kingdom of Kongo between the rival houses of Kinlaza and Kimpanzu, joined by other factions such as the Água Rosada of Kibangu and the da Silva of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soyo">Soyo</a>. It began after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mbwila">Battle of Mbwila</a> in 1665, where the Portuguese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> defeated Kongo and killed King António I and many Kinlaza nobles. Soyo then made and unmade kings, and in 1678 the capital, São <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>, was burnt, leaving rival claimants to rule from mountain fortresses. Pedro IV retook São Salvador in 1709 and set a rotating succession between the two houses, but many Bakongo captured in the fighting had been sold into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> reached its height under King Garcia II, who with his brother Álvaro VI had taken the throne from the House of Kimpanzu and founded the House of Kinlaza, named for the Nlaza kanda. The new dynasty traced its claim through the mother, unlike the paternal descent of the Kimpanzu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsundi">Nsundi</a>, and rival claimants rallied behind the Kimpanzu, who were entrenched in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soyo">Soyo</a>. By the end of Garcia's reign Soyo was fully independent. His son António I, who came to the throne in 1661, set out to drive the Portuguese from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, and a dispute over the border <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> of Mbwila brought the two to battle on 29 October 1665.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After António's death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soyo">Soyo</a>, ruled by the Silva kanda, made itself master of the kingdom. It helped overthrow and kill the hated Álvaro VII, oversaw the election of Álvaro VIII, removed Pedro III in 1669 and installed the Kimpanzu Álvaro IX. In 1670 Rafael I overthrew Álvaro IX and called in an Angolan army, which killed Soyo's Count Estêvão da Silva but was crushed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kitombo">Battle of Kitombo</a> by Prince Pedro da Silva, a defeat that ended Portuguese ambitions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> until the 19th century.</p><p>In 1678 Pedro III marched on São Salvador with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaga">Jaga</a> mercenaries; King Daniel I was killed and most of the city burnt. Pedro III was himself assassinated under a flag of truce in 1680 by Manuel de Nóbrega, Daniel's brother. At Kibangu two brothers of the Água Rosada kanda took power in 1688, and the younger, Pedro IV, slowly won over the claimants. He had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimpa-vita">Beatriz Kimpa Vita</a>, leader of the Antonian movement, burnt for heresy at the urging of Capuchin missionaries, then routed the Antonians at São Salvador on 15 February 1709 and defeated João II at Mbula on 4 October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Pedro IV pardoned the Kimpanzu and made their leading claimant, Manuel Makasa, his son-in-law and heir; he reigned as Manuel II after Pedro's death in 1718, and peace held, with few breaks, for some 50 years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> never regained its former power and broke up into fiefdoms that recognised the king without truly obeying him. Whole populations had been drafted into the armies, and captives of every faction were sold through Loango and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>; over thirty thousand Antonians alone were sold into Atlantic slavery after their defeat in 1709.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kongo Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
